%[text] # The Post
%[text] [![](text:image:6447)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=simulink/blog&project=2026_08_06_crane/crane.prj)
%[text] A few days ago, my colleague Ned Gulley published this blog post: [Simulate in MATLAB, Animate in Blender](https://blogs.mathworks.com/community/2026/07/31/simulate-in-matlab-animate-in-blender/).
%[text] In this post, Ned simulated a sliding gantry crane supporting a heavy swinging load and then controlled it to swing the load above a wall. Here is what the final result looks like:
%[text] ![](text:image:6c64)
%[text] In his post, Ned did not include the code, but mentions that he implemented all that in MATLAB. My first reaction was:
%[text] *Why implementing that in MATLAB when we have Simscape Multibody?*
%[text] So I launched [Codex](https://chatgpt.com/codex/), connected it to MATLAB through the [Simulink Agentic Toolkit](https://github.com/matlab/simulink-agentic-toolkit) and asked it to review Ned's post and implement something similar, but using Simulink and Simscape Multibody.
%[text] I think this makes an interesting story showing the importance of planning carefully before implementation, along with highlighting the kind of things the Simulink Agentic is able to do.
%[text] ## The Plan
%[text] After reading the post, Codex used the [specifying-plant-models](https://github.com/matlab/simulink-agentic-toolkit/tree/main/skills-catalog/model-based-design-core/specifying-plant-models) skill from the Simulink Agentic Toolkit to plan how it was going to address this challenge. 
%[text] It defined a set of goals 
%[text] ![](text:image:23cd)
%[text] It divided the work in 5 phases:
%[text] ![](text:image:0cff)
%[text] Compared to other projects where I used this skill, the proposed plan was relatively light without a lot of details. I decided to leave it like that and begin the implementation.
%[text] ## The Plant Model
%[text] Here is what the model looked like after phase 2. Note that I manually moved and resized a few blocks to get a better screenshot for this blog. I am very picky with Simulink models layout.
%[text] ![](text:image:1ff5)
%[text] Applying a constant force on the base, I could use the [Multibody Explorer](https://www.mathworks.com/help/sm/ref/multibodyexplorer.html) to confirm that it moves the way I expect
%[text] ![](text:image:5211)
%[text] ## Control Design
%[text] If you look in the scripts folder in my project, you will find that a series of scripts that Codex used to design the maneuver to pump and swing the crane.
%[text] ![](text:image:9155)
%[text] Here is what the code designing the LQR controller looks like:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] function K = designLQRCatchController(p)
%[text] %DESIGNLQRCATCHCONTROLLER LQR gain for the small-angle crane model.
%[text] 
%[text] if nargin == 0
%[text]     p = craneParameters();
%[text] end
%[text] 
%[text] M = p.trolleyMass;
%[text] m = p.payloadMass;
%[text] L = p.pendulumLength;
%[text] g = p.gravity;
%[text] 
%[text] % State vector: [x; xdot; theta; thetaDot]. theta is measured from vertical.
%[text] A = [0 1 0 0; ...
%[text]      0 0 -(m*g)/M 0; ...
%[text]      0 0 0 1; ...
%[text]      0 0 ((M+m)*g)/(M*L) 0];
%[text] B = [0; 1/M; 0; -1/(M*L)];
%[text] 
%[text] K = lqr(A, B, p.lqrQ, p.lqrR);
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] I will not go into full details here, but if you're interested, see crane-controller-design-blog.md. Once the design was complete, I asked Codex to write this report of how it implemented to control logic and what each file it created does.
%[text] ## Controller Implementation
%[text] If you look at the implementation plan generated by Codex, there is not much details on how the controller would be implemented. Since I did not make specific requests, it made decisions that are different than what I would have chosen. I think this will be interesting for this post to highlight the iterations I made to get the controller implemented the way I wanted. 
%[text] In Simulink, the first version of the controller implemented by Codex looked like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:8cb7)
%[text] With the Stateflow chart managing the phases of the maneuver
%[text] ![](text:image:7b05)
%[text] and the control equations implemented in a MATLAB Function block.
%[text] While this is not fundamentally wrong, I usually prefer using Simulink math blocks when possible. So I asked Codex to re-implement the Controller Force Law block into a Subsystem with basic math blocks. Here is what I got:
%[text] ![](text:image:0225)
%[text] Not exactly what I was hoping for, but once again, I had not given it very clear instructions of what I wanted. 
%[text] I then took the time to explain in details that I wanted it to implement the control equations inside Simulink States inside the Stateflow chart.
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:17e6)
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:0fbe)
%[text] Here is the final maneuver
%[text] ![](text:image:6c3b)

